  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 425 PM EDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nExpect the same overall pattern to continue Thursday night and\ninto Friday with upper ridging over the Western States and an \nupper trough over the Northeast. Friday will be a tad warmer as\nthe large upper high slowly shifts eastward, but it will still \nquite nice with dew points mostly in the upper 50s to lower 60s.\nIncreasing high level clouds could also help moderate temps, \nbut expect most - if not all - precip to remain W of the area \ncloser to the MS River through the daytime hours Friday.\n\nFriday night the surface high pressure shifts far enough east \nto allow return flow to begin advecting much higher moisture  \n(PWATs near 2\") ahead of a shortwave flowing around the \nperiphery of the upper ridge. This is expected to increase rain \nchances into the day on Saturday. The best chances for rain are\nexpected across southern Kentucky and during the AM hours, but \nthis activity should be fairly benign with severe weather quite\nunlikely. If anything, can't rule out some locally heavy rain\ngiven the aforementioned high PWATs.\n\nThe bigger weather story this weekend and into early next week\nwill likely be the return of considerably warmer and more humid\nconditions. Highs look to return into the 90s perhaps as early\nas Sunday, but definitely for Monday. Dew points will return to\nmore seasonable levels in the 70s. This will allow for heat\nindices to climb to around triple digits Monday afternoon. The \nmagnitude of the heat and humidity may dip a bit towards \nmidweek, which should keep the Heat Risk (cumulative effects) \nlimited to the \"moderate\" category. Occasional chances for \nprecipitation will be introduced next week as waves work towards\nthe Lower Ohio Valley within northwest upper flow, but nothing \nlooks overly concerning at this time.",
